-- js-controlflow.rofl — THE THIRD LAYER, ADDED AS ONE FACT.
--
-- `layer(controlflow).` is the whole declaration, and the programme's central
-- claim is that the model then lights up every place it needs describing. That
-- claim had never been run. It was, before this file had a second line:
--
--     layer(L)                              3  ->    4
--     cell[audit](A, K, S, L)             179  ->  229
--     open_cell[audit](K, S, L)            76  ->  121
--     unqueued[audit](K, S, L)              0  ->   45
--
-- Fifty cells from one fact, forty-five of them work the plan was silent about,
-- each named by kind. Nothing had to be enumerated by hand and nothing could be
-- forgotten, which is the property the matrix exists for.
--
-- The rest of this file is the answer to what that fact enumerated.
layer(controlflow).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT THE LAYER ANSWERS. Six kinds, and the split is by MECHANISM rather than
-- by kind — rules/js-controlflow.rofl carries the vocabulary and the audits
-- that keep it closed.
handled(js, if_statement,               controlflow, r_guard_arm).
handled(js, conditional_expression,     controlflow, r_guard_arm).
handled(js, logical_expression,         controlflow, r_guard_arm).
handled(js, for_of_statement,           controlflow, r_guard_arm).
handled(js, optional_call_expression,   controlflow, r_short_circuit).
handled(js, optional_member_expression, controlflow, r_short_circuit).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT IT USED TO WAIVE, AND THE WAIVER WAS A CLAIM ABOUT THE PROGRAM. Until
-- 2026-09-07 this file read:
--
--   `await` and `yield` transfer control and do NOT guard anything: control
--   comes back, and the code after them runs — later, but it runs. Filing them
--   as guards would have made `may_not_run` report functions that always run.
--
-- The last sentence is TRUE and is not a reason to refuse: `may_not_run` is a
-- may-set whose own comment says it over-covers on purpose, and the dangerous
-- direction is the set being too NARROW. The first sentence is the defect —
-- control comes back only if the awaited thing SETTLES and only if a consumer
-- asks a generator for another value, and neither is something this layer can
-- decide. Every await in the corpus settled, so the reason had never been
-- exercised; a fixture that suspends forever put `afterStall` — a function the
-- model calls and the runtime never enters — in front of the acceptance gate
-- within one run.
handled(js, await_expression, controlflow, r_suspension).
handled(js, yield_expression, controlflow, r_suspension).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT IT LEFT OPEN AND NO LONGER DOES, 2026-09-06 — w_cf_abrupt_transfer.
-- Everything after a `return` in the same statement list is unreachable, which
-- is a fact about ORDER, and until this date nothing in this layer expressed
-- order at all. `ast_child(B, F, I, S)` had carried the index since the scanner
-- was written and no rule had ever read it; `r_abrupt` reads it now.
handled(js, return_statement, controlflow, r_abrupt).

-- A DEFAULTED PARAMETER IS AN ARM SKIPPED BY A CONDITION, 2026-09-08
-- (w_destructuring_rest_and_spread). `function f(n, made = fallbackMaker(n))`
-- calls `fallbackMaker` at exactly the sites that omit the argument, so the
-- `right` of an `assignment_pattern` is a guard arm in the same sense a `?:`
-- alternate is. `guard_kind(assignment_pattern, right)` is the whole rule.
--
-- MEASURED BOTH WAYS. At run time, two calls to the same defaulted function —
-- one supplying the argument and one not — ran the default expression exactly
-- ONCE. In the model, with the fixture in and the row absent, `fallbackMaker`
-- was NOT in `may_not_run` although its only call site is inside a default.
--
-- IT IS THE ONLY ONE OF THE FOUR THIS LAYER CAN ANSWER, and that is measured
-- rather than assumed — see the `object_pattern` paragraph below.
node_kind(js, assignment_pattern).
handled(js, assignment_pattern, controlflow, r_guard_arm).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE SWEEP (queue item w_cf_sweep). Forty cells, and the point of doing it by
-- hand rather than by default is that NINE OF THEM ARE NOT RESIDUE. A default
-- would have called all forty `not_yet` and been wrong about thirty-one; a
-- blanket waiver would have been wrong about nine.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THIRTY-ONE THAT REALLY TRANSFER NOTHING. A literal, a name, a pattern, a
-- specifier: control does not branch at any of them, and the reason is the same
-- sentence for all of them, which is what makes this part arithmetic.
ignored(js, identifier,                  controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, string_literal,              controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, numeric_literal,             controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, boolean_literal,             controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
-- ...and `null_literal`, added 2026-09-06 when `catch { return null; }` entered
-- the corpus with the propagation fixtures and `vocabulary_gap[audit]` named it
-- within one run — the second time in two iterations that gate has caught a kind
-- my own fixture introduced, which is the whole reason it counts rather than
-- reporting nothing.
node_kind(js, null_literal).
ignored(js, null_literal,                controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, template_literal,            controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, tagged_template_expression,  controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, array_expression,            controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
-- ...AND `object_pattern` IS NOT HERE ANY MORE. Its row read
-- `a_no_control_transfer` and 2026-09-08 MEASURED that the reason is false:
-- `const { taken } = withGetter` RUNS the getter, which is a transfer into user
-- code by syntax that hides it — the same class this layer already models under
-- `accessor_call` for `member_expression`. The probe is seven cases and it
-- recorded which functions ran; an object pattern, an array pattern, an object
-- rest and all three spread positions each transferred control, and only
-- `assignment_pattern` was answerable with the machinery this layer has.
--
-- The cell is OPEN and OWNED, by `w_destructuring_hides_a_call`. Second
-- borrowed-reason correction in this file after `import_expression`, and it
-- arrives the same way: the reason was re-READ rather than re-labelled.
ignored(js, object_expression,           controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, object_property,             controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, assignment_expression,       controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, sequence_expression,         controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, parenthesized_expression,    controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, this_expression,             controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, new_expression,              controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, variable_declarator,         controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, class_declaration,           controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
-- `decorator` LEFT THIS LIST ON 2026-09-08 and its row is now a `handled` in
-- the block below. `a_no_control_transfer` was written when no corpus could
-- contain a decorator, and it is false: a decorator IS a call.
ignored(js, class_accessor_property,     controlflow, a_both_accessors_are_synthesised).
ignored(js, import_declaration,          controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, import_specifier,            controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, import_default_specifier,    controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, import_namespace_specifier,  controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, export_named_declaration,    controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, export_default_declaration,  controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, export_all_declaration,      controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).

-- THE TWO EXPORT SPECIFIER FORMS, 2026-09-08 (w_export_specifier_forms), and
-- they get OPPOSITE verdicts — which is the whole reason the item is two kinds
-- and not one.
--
-- `export { f }` and `export { f as g }` PUT f ON THE ENTRY SURFACE, and until
-- this iteration nothing did: `exported_fn` walks the CONTAINMENT of an export
-- declaration, and a specifier list contains two identifiers and no function.
-- MEASURED on a probe whose three functions were exported only that way:
-- `exported_fn` 0, `entry_point` 0, `reachable` 0, `no_entry_point[audit]`
-- firing on the file, and `may_not_be_reached` naming `helper` — a function
-- that same file exports AND calls. The positive control, the identical three
-- functions written `export function`, gives all three as entry points and an
-- empty `may_not_be_reached`. So this cell was not merely unmodelled; it was
-- WRONG in the dangerous direction, and the site is
-- test/fixtures/js/export-surface.js.
node_kind(js, export_specifier).
handled(js, export_specifier, controlflow, r_export_entry_surface).

-- ...AND THE NAMESPACE FORM INTRODUCES NO LOCAL ENTRY, which is the same
-- sentence `export_all_declaration` carries three lines up and it is MEASURED
-- rather than inherited: the scanner emits an `exported` child for an
-- ExportNamespaceSpecifier and NO `local` child at all, so there is no name in
-- this module for the entry surface to reach. What `export * as ns from './m'`
-- offers is m's own export surface under one name, and that is m's entry
-- surface, derived where m is.
node_kind(js, export_namespace_specifier).
ignored(js, export_namespace_specifier, controlflow, a_no_local_binding_to_enter).
ignored(js, tsas_expression,             controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, tsnon_null_expression,       controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, ts_string_keyword,           controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
-- ...AND TWO THAT INHERITED A REASON THAT TURNED OUT TO BE FALSE. They read
-- `a_control_returns_so_the_site_still_runs` — `await`'s waiver — under a
-- comment saying `a dynamic import suspends, and the site runs`. When that
-- waiver was refuted on 2026-09-07 these two were re-read rather than
-- re-labelled, and the borrowed reason was wrong about the construct as well:
-- `import(p)` does NOT suspend. It evaluates to a promise, like any other
-- expression that produces a value; whatever suspends is the `await` around it,
-- and this layer models that now. So the honest reason is the one every other
-- value-producing form here carries.
ignored(js, import_expression,           controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, import,                      controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- NINE THAT ARE NOT RESIDUE, and each carries the reason it is open rather than
-- the default. They fall into three questions, and none of the three is
-- member-expression-sized: each is an item.
--
-- FIVE FUNCTION FORMS — a body is a CONTROL REGION, entered only by a call, and
-- everything in it runs only if the function is entered. Answered 2026-09-06 by
-- w_cf_reachability. This paragraph used to say the walk was declared absent
-- because "the transitive question wants an ENTRY POINT, and a model that
-- guessed at entry points would answer something nobody asked" — and the
-- measurement that closed it said something sharper: written WITHOUT an entry
-- point the question is not under-determined, it is UNSTRATIFIED, and the
-- kernel refuses the program outright. The entry point is what makes the
-- question askable, and it is not guessed: a module's EXPORT SURFACE is its
-- entry surface, which is a fact about the language and already on the store.
handled(js, function_declaration,        controlflow, r_reachability).
handled(js, function_expression,         controlflow, r_reachability).
handled(js, arrow_function_expression,   controlflow, r_reachability).
handled(js, class_method,                controlflow, r_reachability).

-- ...AND `class_expression`, 2026-09-08 (w_class_expression), which gets
-- `handled` rather than the `a_no_control_transfer` its DECLARATION twin
-- carries thirty lines above — and the difference is measured, not stylistic.
--
-- TWO THINGS A CLASS EXPRESSION DOES AT THIS LAYER, both with a site in
-- test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt:
--
--   THE ENTRY SURFACE. `export const Bracket = class { fasten(n) {...} }` puts
--   `fasten` on it. `exported_fn` walks CONTAINMENT and asks `not in_fn`, and a
--   method of an exported class expression has no enclosing function — so it is
--   an entry point on exactly the same footing as a method of an exported class
--   declaration. Measured: `entry_point[code]` contains `fasten`, and `turn` —
--   a method of a class expression bound to a module-local `const` — is
--   correctly NOT on it.
--
--   THE HERITAGE CLAUSE RUNS. `class extends mountOf() {}` calls `mountOf` when
--   the class expression is EVALUATED, which for an expression is wherever the
--   expression stands. Measured: `calls_in` carries `shapes.ts top -> mountOf`.
--   So `a_no_control_transfer` would have been FALSE for this kind, which is the
--   same correction `object_pattern` earned in this file the same week and the
--   reason that one is re-read rather than re-labelled. The declaration twin has
--   the identical exposure and its row is NOT changed here — it belongs to
--   whoever owns that cell, and it is recorded as a finding instead —
--   f_a_heritage_clause_runs_and_a_class_is_waived_as_transferring_nothing.
handled(js, class_expression,            controlflow, r_reachability).
node_kind(js, class_expression).

-- `meta_property` IS INERT AT THIS LAYER, 2026-09-08 (w_meta_property), and
-- this is the ONE of its four cells that one verdict can honestly cover — which
-- is the item's whole question, since `new.target` and `import.meta` are two
-- constructs sharing a node kind.
--
-- MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED, because `a_no_control_transfer` is the reason
-- this file has already had to withdraw once. A `meta_property` node has
-- exactly two children and both are identifiers — `meta` and `property` — so
-- there is nothing inside it that could be a `site[code]`, no callee position,
-- no accessor read and no expression to evaluate. Both forms are LEAVES: the
-- syntax is two tokens joined by a dot and neither token is a lookup.
--
-- WHAT IT DOES NOT WAIVE, and the difference matters: `new.target` DISTINGUISHES
-- TWO WAYS OF ENTERING a function — `f()` gives undefined and `new f()` gives
-- the constructor — and this model has no notion of call MODE at all, so a
-- function with two entry modes has one body and one answer here. That is a
-- real gap and it is a VALUE gap, which is why the dataflow cell is left open
-- and owned rather than waived beside this one.
ignored(js, meta_property,               controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
node_kind(js, meta_property).
handled(js, object_method,               controlflow, r_reachability).

-- TWO CALL FORMS — a call transfers control INTO the callee and may leave by
-- THROWING rather than returning. Answered 2026-09-06 by w_exception_flow.
-- `always_throws` is decidable from syntax in exactly one case (no `return`
-- anywhere, a `throw` at the top level of the body), and a call to such a
-- function is an abrupt transfer for the rest of ITS OWN function — a `try`
-- stops it, and so does the function boundary, beyond which propagation is
-- w_exn_propagation's question. `super` is the same rule reached through
-- `ctor_of[flow]`, because a super call resolves to the CLASS and not to the
-- body that runs.
handled(js, call_expression, controlflow, r_call_is_an_exit).
handled(js, super,           controlflow, r_call_is_an_exit).

-- TWO MEMBER FORMS — a property read is not a transfer UNLESS the property is
-- an accessor, and a getter is a call wearing a read's syntax. Answered
-- 2026-09-06 by w_cf_accessor, and the note that stood here said telling them
-- apart "needs the property to be known as an accessor, which is a fact about
-- the object and not about the site" — true, and the fact was already on the
-- store: the scanner emits `ast_attr(M, kind, "get")` for every method and the
-- value layer already reads that attribute for `"constructor"`. Nothing had
-- ever asked it for `"get"`.
handled(js, member_expression, controlflow, r_accessor).
handled(js, decorator, controlflow, r_decorator_call).
-- ...AND ITS OPTIONAL TWIN CANNOT SAY THIS, which is a real loss and worth the
-- sentence. `optional_member_expression` is already MODELLED at this layer for
-- its short-circuit, and a kind-level cell at a layer with no refinement column
-- is one word — so `o?.p` where `p` is an accessor is a control transfer this
-- model cannot report without un-ticking the one it already has. Written as a
-- claim it raised `queue_stale[audit]` immediately, which is the matrix
-- refusing to hold two verdicts for one cell.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE FOUR KINDS THE FRAME ADDED, 2026-09-06 (w_vocabulary_frame). The kind is
-- re-declared here because a file that makes a claim declares the kind — the
-- convention w_vocabulary_home exists to question, and the one in force.
node_kind(js, binary_expression).
node_kind(js, unary_expression).
node_kind(js, class_body).
node_kind(js, template_element).

ignored(js, binary_expression, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, unary_expression, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, class_body, controlflow, a_a_container_transfers_nothing).
ignored(js, template_element, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).

-- TEN TYPE NODES, 2026-09-07 (w_type_surface). Erased before anything runs,
-- so there is no point at which control could reach one.
node_kind(js, tsfunction_type).
node_kind(js, tsnull_keyword).
node_kind(js, tsnumber_keyword).
node_kind(js, tsstring_keyword).
node_kind(js, tstype_alias_declaration).
node_kind(js, tstype_annotation).
node_kind(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation).
node_kind(js, tstype_query).
node_kind(js, tstype_reference).
node_kind(js, tsunion_type).

ignored(js, tsfunction_type,                controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsnull_keyword,                 controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsnumber_keyword,               controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsstring_keyword,               controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_alias_declaration,       controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_annotation,              controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation, controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_query,                   controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_reference,               controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsunion_type,                   controlflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THREE VALUE FORMS THE VOCABULARY NEVER NAMED, 2026-09-08
-- (w_update_and_literals). All three are waived here, and the item's real
-- control-flow work is NOT in these three rows — it is the
-- `guard_kind(for_statement, update)` row in rules/js-controlflow.rofl, which
-- this item found because `i++` is what a for-update is written as and nobody
-- had looked at the slot while the form was outside the vocabulary.
--
-- THE WAIVERS ARE ABOUT THE FORM AND NOT ABOUT THE POSITION, which is the
-- distinction that keeps them honest: this layer's per-kind cell asks whether
-- a kind TRANSFERS control, and `guarded` answers separately for whatever is
-- written in a skippable place. None of the three transfers anything. An
-- update runs its operand's ToNumeric and writes back; a bigint and a regexp
-- literal are values. `may_throw[code]` here is the explicit-throw model —
-- `throw` statements and calls that reach one — so the implicit TypeError a
-- `const` update raises is outside what this layer models for EVERY kind, not
-- for these three.
node_kind(js, update_expression).
node_kind(js, big_int_literal).
node_kind(js, reg_exp_literal).

ignored(js, update_expression, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, big_int_literal,   controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
ignored(js, reg_exp_literal,   controlflow, a_no_control_transfer).
-- ==========================================================================
-- ES2022 CLASS SYNTAX, 2026-09-08 — queue item w_class_fields.
node_kind(js, class_property).
node_kind(js, class_private_property).
node_kind(js, class_private_method).
node_kind(js, private_name).
node_kind(js, static_block).

-- A FIELD INITIALISER IS AN ARM SKIPPED BY THE ABSENCE OF A `new`, and the
-- SEVENTH mechanism in `transfer_mechanism` is what keeps that from being filed
-- under one of the six that would be wrong. `per_construction` is not
-- `skip_arm` — nothing near a field branches — and it is not
-- `loop_at_least_once` either, because a class that is never constructed runs
-- its instance initialisers zero times.
--
-- MEASURED BY RUNNING THE SHAPE, and it split one kind into two answers:
-- `class Never { unused = mark(); static tag = mark(); }`, defined and never
-- constructed, ran the STATIC initialiser and not the instance one. So
-- `field_init` guards the non-static half only, and the model agrees: with the
-- fixture in, `may_not_run` names `stamped minted inked scored struck burnished`
-- and NOT `sealed`, which is the static field's callee.
handled(js, class_property,         controlflow, r_field_guard).
handled(js, class_private_property, controlflow, r_field_guard).

-- A PRIVATE METHOD IS A FUNCTION LIKE ANY OTHER at this layer: it is in
-- `fn_kind`, so `may_not_run` and `may_not_be_reached` range over it. The site
-- is the private GETTER, read behind a conditional in the fixture, and
-- `may_not_run[code]` names its node.
handled(js, class_private_method,   controlflow, r_reachability).

-- READING A PRIVATE NAME MAY TRANSFER CONTROL, because `get #face()` is a
-- `class_private_method` carrying `kind: "get"` and `this.#face` runs it. The
-- public arm of `accessor_read` cannot reach it — a private name never touches
-- `member_value` or `selects` — so the private accessor needed an arm of its
-- own, and it needs NO receiver literal: the class body that declares `#face` is
-- the only place `this.#face` can be written.
handled(js, private_name,           controlflow, r_accessor).

-- A STATIC BLOCK RUNS UNCONDITIONALLY WHEN THE CLASS DEFINITION IS EVALUATED —
-- measured by running the shape, where it fires before any `new` and once. So
-- it guards nothing, skips nothing and suspends nothing, and the reachability of
-- what is inside it is already the reachability of the class declaration, which
-- the enclosing guards decide. The one thing it does need is for the calls
-- inside it to count as module-level entry points, and `reachable[code]`'s
-- `not in_fn` arm already says so without a rule about this kind.
ignored(js, static_block,           controlflow, a_a_static_block_runs_when_the_class_is_defined).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FOUR KINDS WHOSE SURFACE IS A BINDING AND WHOSE SEMANTICS IS A CALL
-- (w_destructuring_hides_a_call, 2026-09-08). Three of the four were
-- `not_modelled` / `not_yet` and `object_pattern` had carried
-- `a_no_control_transfer` since the forty-cell sweep, borrowed from
-- `array_expression` where it is true.
--
-- WHAT THE ITEM WAITED ON WAS THE WRONG THING, and that is the result worth
-- keeping. It was blocked on `w_env_api_surface` because naming the receiver's
-- iterator reads as a standard-library question. MEASURED against the surface
-- that item generated, with a positive control on the same query shape:
-- `lib_member(P, K, R)` carries 130 rows over eight prototypes, and
-- `lib_member(P, "iterator", R)`, `lib_member(P, "Symbol.iterator", R)` and
-- `lib_member(P, "next", R)` are each ZERO — the surface is keyed by NAME and a
-- well-known symbol is not a name.
--
-- THE REAL DISCRIMINATOR IS THE RECEIVER. Ten cases run against V8's own stack:
-- an array pattern, an array spread and a spread argument over a PLAIN ARRAY run
-- no user code at all, and an object spread over a PLAIN OBJECT runs none
-- either — while the same four syntaxes over a source that owns a getter or a
-- `[Symbol.iterator]` each transfer control, and V8 attributes both the iterator
-- and its `next` to the ENCLOSING function exactly as it does for `for-of`. So
-- the question was never the standard library: it was that every destructuring
-- site in this corpus read a plain source, and no receiver here could
-- contradict the sentence.
--
-- TWO RULES AND NOT ONE, and `spread_element` carries both. In `properties` a
-- spread copies keys and runs getters; in `elements` or `arguments` it exhausts
-- an iterator. Those are two mechanisms, and this layer's own doctrine is that
-- mechanisms sharing a bucket get one verdict between them.
handled(js, object_pattern,  controlflow, r_destructure_accessor).
handled(js, rest_element,    controlflow, r_destructure_accessor).
handled(js, spread_element,  controlflow, r_destructure_accessor).
handled(js, spread_element,  controlflow, r_destructure_iterator).
handled(js, array_pattern,   controlflow, r_destructure_iterator).

-- ...AND ALL FOUR CELLS ARE `checked`, which is the first time this layer can
-- say so. The oracle is the same execution oracle the call graph uses — V8's own
-- stack, read by test/fixtures/js-call/trace.mjs, which reads no rule and no
-- scanner fact — and the fixture sites are counted per kind and per mechanism.
-- Every one of these edges is in the model AND in the oracle, with `missed`
-- empty over the whole corpus.
checked(js, object_pattern,  controlflow, r_destructure_accessor, o_run_with_stack, 2).
checked(js, rest_element,    controlflow, r_destructure_accessor, o_run_with_stack, 1).
checked(js, spread_element,  controlflow, r_destructure_accessor, o_run_with_stack, 1).
checked(js, spread_element,  controlflow, r_destructure_iterator, o_run_with_stack, 2).
checked(js, array_pattern,   controlflow, r_destructure_iterator, o_run_with_stack, 1).
